South Korea's president will visit  president Trump next week to report on  his latest summit with Kim jong-un moon  jaein wrapped up a second day of talks  with the North Korean leader in  Pyongyang this morning their third  meeting of the year produced a series of  pledges on issues from reducing military  tensions to the Olympics South Korea  says Kim is willing to take more steps  towards denuclearization Ben Tracy  returned from North Korea last week and  is tracking this summit from Beijing  been good morning good morning so these  two leaders agreed to quite a bit the  big question now is how much of it will  actually happen and will the u.s. go  along with it now in this latest  declaration North Korea agrees to  permanently shutter its main missile  engine testing site and its launch pad  now this is where the North has tested  several of its intercontinental  ballistic missiles what's significant  here is that Kim Jong moon says he will  allow outside experts to come in and  verify that North Korea has actually  dismantled it the North also says it's  willing to shudder its main nuclear  facilities in Jung Byung but only if the  United States makes some unspecified  reciprocal action North Korea has made  it clear it will not unilaterally  denuclearize now we were in North Korea  last week as the regime marked the  anniversary of North Korea's founding  they did not parade their  intercontinental ballistic missiles or  even mention nuclear weapons at any of  their events but despite what North  Korea is doing and saying publicly  satellite imagery and reports from the  UN show that they are still moving  forward with their nuclear weapons  program they're just doing it much more  quietly Gayle all right Ben Tracy  reporting from Beijing
 

